WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.180
Here is the first committed step drawn as simple beads on a string.

2
00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:13.380
Ribulose 1,5-bisphosphate is a 5-carbon backbone capped at each end by a

3
00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:19.580
phosphate. The enzyme Rubisco grabs a single molecule of carbon dioxide and attaches

4
00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:23.640
it to that 5-carbon chain, briefly making a 6-carbon intermediate.

5
00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:27.660
That intermediate is unstable and immediately splits down the middle.

6
00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:32.720
The result is two identical 3-carbon molecules of 3-phosphoglycerate.

7
00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:39.400
Notice the bookkeeping, 1,5-carbon sugar plus 1 carbon dioxide yields 6 carbons

8
00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:42.380
total, which divide cleanly into two 3's.

9
00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:46.880
Counting carbons this way is the key to following the entire cycle without getting

10
00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:47.880
lost.

11
00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:51.560
Let us total the energy bill as a pyramid built from the bottom up.

12
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:54.820
At the base, one carbon dioxide enters.

13
00:00:55.480 --> 00:01:02.520
Three turns of the cycle, fixing three carbon dioxides, cost nine ATP and six NADPH,

14
00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:06.840
and yield one net molecule of glyceraldehyde three phosphate.

15
00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:12.420
To assemble a full six carbon glucose, you must run the cycle six times, doubling

16
00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:16.840
everything. 18 ATP and 12 NADPH per glucose.

17
00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:21.500
Breaking it down by phase, fixation by Rubisco is energetically free.

18
00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:27.840
The reduction step that turns acid into sugar costs the ATP and NADPH, and

19
00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:31.360
regenerating ribulose bisphosphate costs additional ATP.

20
00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:36.740
Sugar is expensive, and this ledger shows exactly where every high energy molecule

21
00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:37.740
is spent.

22
00:01:38.133 --> 00:01:41.573
Follow the dominoes that fall when a plant gets too hot.

23
00:01:42.073 --> 00:01:46.153
Temperature rises, so the plant closes its stomata to conserve water.

24
00:01:46.813 --> 00:01:48.853
But closed stomata trap the leaf.

25
00:01:49.513 --> 00:01:55.173
Fresh carbon dioxide can no longer enter, and internal carbon dioxide drops while

26
00:01:55.173 --> 00:02:00.433
oxygen builds up. Now oxygen starts winning the competition for Rubisco's active

27
00:02:00.433 --> 00:02:03.873
site and the enzyme misfires as an oxygenase.

28
00:02:04.273 --> 00:02:09.213
That wasteful oxygenase reaction burns carbon and energy for no sugar gained.

29
00:02:09.853 --> 00:02:14.113
Each step forces the next and the chain ends in lost productivity.

30
00:02:15.013 --> 00:02:20.033
This single cascade explains why hot, dry climates are so hard on ordinary plants,

31
00:02:20.393 --> 00:02:24.773
and it sets up the two evolutionary fixes, spatial and temporal.

32
00:02:25.617 --> 00:02:30.237
Compare two runs of the cycle side by side, counting carbons the whole way.

33
00:02:30.757 --> 00:02:37.257
On top, the normal cycle, six carbon dioxides feed in, Rubisco makes 12 molecules of

34
00:02:37.257 --> 00:02:45.057
3-PGA, which become 12G3P, 10G3P regenerate the starting sugars, two exit, and you

35
00:02:45.057 --> 00:02:48.517
net one glucose, a gain of six fixed carbons.

36
00:02:48.517 --> 00:02:56.077
On the bottom, photo respiration, Rubisco grabs oxygen instead, producing 3-PGA plus

37
00:02:56.077 --> 00:03:01.217
phosphoglycolate. The salvage pathway recovers some carbon but releases carbon

38
00:03:01.217 --> 00:03:04.937
dioxide, and you actually lose three fixed carbons.

39
00:03:05.457 --> 00:03:09.537
There are not even enough carbons left to regenerate the starting sugar, and no

40
00:03:09.537 --> 00:03:13.357
glucose is made. The contrast makes the cost unmistakable.
